WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.869 --> 00:00:17.275
Captain Farragut was a good seaman, worthy of the fine ship that he commanded.

2
00:00:18.176 --> 00:00:23.259
He believed in the gigantic narwhal and would not let anyone onboard question its existence.

3
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:27.421
The creature was real, and he had sworn to rid the seas of it.

4
00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:33.264
Either Captain Farragut would kill the narwhal, or the narwhal would kill Captain Farragut.

5
00:00:33.952 --> 00:00:35.232
There was no third option.

6
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:39.474
The officers onboard the Abraham Lincoln shared their captain's opinion.

7
00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:44.495
They constantly discussed the narwhal, calculating the chances of meeting it.

8
00:00:45.355 --> 00:00:50.537
As for the crew, they wanted nothing more than to hunt down the sea unicorn, harpoon it, and

9
00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:51.497
hoist it onboard.

10
00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:58.459
To add to the excitement, Captain Farragut offered a reward of two thousand dollars to the first man

11
00:00:58.619 --> 00:00:59.699
who spotted the creature.

12
00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:04.248
All eyes—including mine—were on the sea.

13
00:01:05.108 --> 00:01:10.390
Only one person onboard was indifferent to the chase and the reward, and that was Conseil.

14
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.751
Captain Farragut had provided his ship with everything necessary to catch the gigantic narwhal.

15
00:01:16.492 --> 00:01:18.492
No whaling ship had ever been better equipped.

16
00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:23.914
But the Abraham Lincoln's best asset was Ned Land, the king of harpooners.

17
00:01:25.074 --> 00:01:27.295
Ned had been born in Canada forty years ago.

18
00:01:28.175 --> 00:01:31.896
Quick, cool, and cunning, he had no equal with the harpoon.

19
00:01:32.808 --> 00:01:37.471
Originally from Quebec, the French-speaking part of Canada, Ned took a liking to me.

20
00:01:38.471 --> 00:01:41.793
Little by little, this quiet man and I got to know each other quite well.

21
00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:44.915
I loved to hear him tell of his adventures on the ocean.

22
00:01:45.896 --> 00:01:49.077
So what was his opinion on the question of the marine monster?

23
00:01:49.858 --> 00:01:53.140
I suspected that he did not believe in the sea unicorn.

24
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.123
By the end of June, we had crossed the Atlantic and would soon reach Cape Horn at the

25
00:01:59.163 --> 00:02:00.604
southern tip of South America.

26
00:02:01.628 --> 00:02:06.791
Sitting on the deck with Ned, I led the conversation around to the topic of the gigantic narwhal.

27
00:02:07.691 --> 00:02:10.233
I examined the chances of success for our mission.

28
00:02:10.253 --> 00:02:14.635
"But you don't believe in the existence of this creature, do you?"

29
00:02:14.995 --> 00:02:15.276
I said.

30
00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:17.797
"Is there any particular reason for that?"

31
00:02:18.798 --> 00:02:21.619
The harpooner stared at me for a few moments before answering.

32
00:02:21.639 --> 00:02:24.641
"Perhaps there is, Professor Aronnax."

33
00:02:24.661 --> 00:02:30.304
"But, Ned, you're a whaler by profession, familiar with all the great marine mammals.

34
00:02:31.116 --> 00:02:33.378
You should be the last person to doubt its existence."

35
00:02:34.298 --> 00:02:39.922
"As a whaler, I've followed many large sea creatures and harpooned a great number of them," he said.

36
00:02:39.942 --> 00:02:47.387
"But no matter how strong they were, neither their tails nor their tusks could have even scratched the

37
00:02:47.487 --> 00:02:49.768
iron plates of a steamship."

38
00:02:49.788 --> 00:02:54.371
I pointed out that there were reports of ships' hulls being pierced by the tusks of narwhals.

39
00:02:54.391 --> 00:02:57.654
"Wooden ships," Ned said carefully.

40
00:02:57.674 --> 00:03:00.896
"That might be possible, though I've never seen it happen.

41
00:03:01.936 --> 00:03:08.102
So until I have further proof, I refuse to believe that whales or sea unicorns could ever produce

42
00:03:08.142 --> 00:03:09.263
the effect you describe."

43
00:03:10.204 --> 00:03:13.947
I told him my theory that the creature might have come from unknown depths in the sea.

44
00:03:14.908 --> 00:03:19.893
Using mathematical equations, I explained how water pressure increases as water gets deeper.

45
00:03:19.913 --> 00:03:26.479
"Its body would have to be strong enough to resist water pressure equal to tens of millions of

46
00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:32.164
pounds." "Then this creature must be made of iron plates that are eight inches thick, like an armored

47
00:03:32.224 --> 00:03:33.925
warship!" exclaimed Ned.

48
00:03:33.945 --> 00:03:40.051
"Think what destruction such a creature would cause if it hurled itself against a ship's hull at the

49
00:03:40.091 --> 00:03:41.332
speed of an express train."

50
00:03:41.352 --> 00:03:48.458
"Yes, well perhaps," Ned replied, shaken by these thoughts but not yet willing to give in.

51
00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:52.261
"So have I convinced you that such a creature exists?"

52
00:03:52.742 --> 00:03:53.102
I asked.

53
00:03:53.122 --> 00:03:58.494
"You've convinced me of one thing: that if this animal lives at the bottom of the sea, it

54
00:03:58.574 --> 00:04:00.255
must be as strong as you say."

55
00:04:00.275 --> 00:04:05.279
"But if it doesn't exist, how do you explain what happened to the Scotia?"

56
00:04:05.299 --> 00:04:11.823
"It Ned hesitated and then blurted out, "I explain it by pronouncing my conviction that it just couldn't

57
00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:12.403
be true!"

58
00:04:13.404 --> 00:04:14.785
I stopped trying to convince Ned.

59
00:04:15.486 --> 00:04:18.568
Our ship sailed around Cape Horn and on to the Pacific.

60
00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:24.692
By the end of July we had reached the northern Pacific, where the creature was last reported.

61
00:04:25.612 --> 00:04:28.534
The entire ship's crew was full of nervous excitement.

62
00:04:29.274 --> 00:04:31.135
They could not eat; they could not sleep.

63
00:04:31.595 --> 00:04:34.257
Twenty times a day someone thought he spotted our prey.

64
00:04:35.157 --> 00:04:39.280
For three more months the Abraham Lincoln sailed the waters of the northern Pacific.

65
00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:45.923
The days seemed to last forever as we explored every part of the ocean between Japan and North

66
00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:47.944
America. We found nothing.

67
00:04:48.945 --> 00:04:54.268
No ship's crew had ever worked harder; the men did everything they could to find the gigantic narwhal.

68
00:04:55.148 --> 00:04:59.392
But now the crew members were calling themselves fools for believing in such a creature.

69
00:05:00.292 --> 00:05:04.356
Every man wanted to catch up on his eating and sleeping, to make up for the time he

70
00:05:04.396 --> 00:05:06.037
had so stupidly sacrificed.

71
00:05:06.838 --> 00:05:09.520
There was only one thing left to do: go home.

72
00:05:11.042 --> 00:05:15.385
The men took this demand to Captain Farragut, but he refused to abandon the search.

73
00:05:16.386 --> 00:05:21.530
I won't say that they threatened to mutiny, but after their appeal to Farragut, they didn't perform their

74
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.092
duties as well as they could have.

75
00:05:24.008 --> 00:05:29.490
At last Captain Farragut said that if the creature did not appear within three days, the pilot would

76
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.050
turn the ship's wheel toward home.

77
00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.812
Captain Farragut made this promise on November 2, and it rallied the crew.

78
00:05:36.612 --> 00:05:38.752
Everyone watched the ocean with renewed attention.

79
00:05:39.753 --> 00:05:44.194
Two days passed during which a thousand schemes were tried to attract the creature.

80
00:05:45.194 --> 00:05:50.296
Large quantities of bacon were trailed behind the ship, but that only succeeded in drawing sharks.

81
00:05:51.276 --> 00:05:55.258
When the ship stopped, men went out in small boats to continue their search.

82
00:05:56.198 --> 00:06:00.780
But November 4 arrived, and still the creature had not made an appearance.

83
00:06:02.381 --> 00:06:05.162
The deadline would expire at noon the next day.

84
00:06:06.202 --> 00:06:07.062
Night was approaching.

85
00:06:07.823 --> 00:06:10.144
Large clouds veiled the face of the moon.

86
00:06:10.964 --> 00:06:13.505
The sea rocked gently under the stern of the vessel.

87
00:06:14.445 --> 00:06:16.706
At that moment I was leaning over the railing.

88
00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:20.428
Conseil, standing near me, was looking straight ahead.

89
00:06:21.364 --> 00:06:25.545
The crew, perched in the rigging, was scanning the horizon in the growing darkness.

90
00:06:25.565 --> 00:06:27.986
"Come, Conseil," I said.

91
00:06:28.006 --> 00:06:31.627
"This is your last chance to earn the two thousand dollars."

92
00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:36.328
"If you will permit me to say this, I never counted on getting the prize."

93
00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.289
"You were right, Conseil," I admitted.

94
00:06:39.309 --> 00:06:41.970
"This was a foolish affair after all.

95
00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.251
We should have returned to France months ago."

96
00:06:44.271 --> 00:06:50.816
"You would be in your little apartment and in the museum," replied Conseil.

97
00:06:50.836 --> 00:06:53.258
"I would have already classified all of your fossils.

98
00:06:53.738 --> 00:06:58.602
And the wild swine would have been drawing crowds to its cage at the zoo in the botanical

99
00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:00.503
gardens." "You're right.

100
00:07:00.863 --> 00:07:02.344
We'll be laughed at for this adventure."

101
00:07:02.364 --> 00:07:05.586
"That's for sure," Conseil said quietly.

102
00:07:05.606 --> 00:07:07.668
"I think people will make fun of you.

103
00:07:08.268 --> 00:07:11.910
And I hope you don't mind my saying so, Master, but you deserve it."

104
00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:19.976
"What!" "You're a very smart man, so you shouldn't expose yourself to—" Just then someone started shouting.

105
00:07:20.796 --> 00:07:21.697
It was Ned Land.

106
00:07:22.678 --> 00:07:23.800
"Look! Over there!"

107
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:24.701
cried Ned.

108
00:07:24.721 --> 00:07:27.324
"It's the very thing we've been searching for!"
